DESCRIPTION

  A reimplementation of the part of cqueues that lua-http uses, written
  in Lua over luaposix.  This is not vendored code.  There is no
  upstream to re-import from, and scripts/luaext.py does not generate
  it.

  It provides the module names lua-http requires, which are cqueues,
  cqueues.socket, cqueues.condition, cqueues.errno and cqueues.auxlib,
  plus cqueues.socket.tls, which bridges an openssl.ssl.context onto the
  mbedtls engine in third_party/luatls.

WHY NOT VENDOR UPSTREAM

  cqueues' event core, src/lib/kpoll.c, has backends for epoll, kqueue
  and Solaris ports, and for nothing else.  Cosmopolitan exposes none of
  those on purpose, because none of them is portable to every platform
  an APE runs on.  Vendoring cqueues would mean first writing a fourth
  backend over poll(2).

  Its socket layer is also wired directly to OpenSSL, including
  <openssl/ssl.h> for starttls, so keeping it and using the mbedtls
  already in this tree are contradictory goals.

  And it is about 26,000 lines of C, most of which lua-http never
  touches: its own DNS resolver, pthread-backed threads, inotify and
  kqueue file notification, signal handling.  What lua-http actually
  uses is ten module functions, one helper from auxlib, and 26 socket
  methods.

WHAT IS NOT HERE

  Threads, the DNS resolver, file notification and signal handling.
  Name resolution goes through luaposix's getaddrinfo, which blocks, so
  a slow lookup stalls every coroutine in the scheduler.  That is
  invisible to a server, which never resolves, and fine for a client
  doing one request at a time.

  Readiness checking is poll(2), which is linear in the number of
  descriptors where cqueues on Linux would have been constant.  That is
  a platform limit rather than a design choice.

NOTES

  The semantics that are easy to get wrong, and that lua-http depends
  on, are documented at the point in the code where they matter: the
  error convention of accept, negative byte counts as non-greedy reads,
  the "*h" MIME header format, the buffering letters on xwrite, end of
  file as a bare nil, eager connect, and controller nesting.  Read those
  comments before changing any of it.
